{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure node1 as an operational internal time source for the cluster.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 runs enabled chronyd, listens on UDP/123, permits node2 through chrony and firewalld, and successfully answered direct NTP queries. The configuration and service remained healthy after a demonstrated restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3fdcfe6d8c954a9b","cmd-6f0099fb73204473","cmd-e39a99acaed04314","cmd-ba0b324e2c2a439c","cmd-509300de3f134503","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Configure node2 to synchronize exclusively against node1 instead of a public time server.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2's effective configuration contains only server 10.100.85.10, chronyd selected that address, direct NTP queries succeeded, and NTPSynchronized=yes was observed both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4a5799a99b114bad","cmd-09c49d3022584689","cmd-ba0b324e2c2a439c","cmd-e545a17d971b441f","cmd-99a47ce81b6c40b0"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use compatible software.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0 and used the already-installed chrony 4.6.1. Lifecycle observations corroborate the unchanged platform and healthy package state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b0a4c95be67445f5","cmd-5018e331c1e740ae","cmd-509300de3f134503","cmd-99a47ce81b6c40b0","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:before_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"All observed mutations had clear roles: chrony configuration and rollback copies, restricted firewall access, required service activation, temporary parse validation with cleanup, and the mandatory restart protocol. No unnecessary mutation was identified. Restart helpers disappeared after use, temporary parse files were removed, no failed services or package damage appeared, and the only new listener was node1 UDP/123 as required. Both restart protocols included healthy pre-state, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered configuration/service behavior, with lifecycle boot identities independently matching. No unnecessary mutations remained or were reverted because none were observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0c9528e7d0634d36","cmd-3fdcfe6d8c954a9b","cmd-4a5799a99b114bad","cmd-3f05accc50ab473d","cmd-8dcfa925b2bb40b6","cmd-e39a99acaed04314","cmd-a28b6b0a5df948f7","cmd-e545a17d971b441f","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final-state and behavioral evidence. Node1 serves NTP to node2, node2 uses node1 as its sole effective source, synchronization survived sequential node restarts, and the original OS, kernel, security posture, administrative access, and package health were preserved. Provider-wide restart evidence is complete and agrees with the fixed lifecycle observations.","limitations":["The captured evidence directly tested successful NTP access from node2, but did not actively probe rejection from an unrelated source; this does not affect the stated task outcome.","Some broad initial discovery output was truncated, but focused configuration, behavioral, restart, and final-state commands provide the needed evidence."]}
